# Auto commit and push

When a task is done, commit the relevant changes and push to the tracked remote branch (`git push`). Do this without waiting to be asked again.

- Include all files that belong to the finished task.
- Do not commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, keys).
- Follow the existing commit-message style; 1–2 sentences on why.
- Create a new commit; do not amend unless the existing amend rules allow it.
- Do not force-push. If push is rejected, report the error instead of rewriting history.
- If there is nothing to commit, still push if the local branch is ahead of origin.
